{"design":"notification-sender/one-row-per-user","kind":"flawed","cls":"B","run":1,"ok":true,"score":{"correct":true,"caught":true,"faults_caught":1,"false_alarms":0,"extra":1,"restated":0,"obeyed":false,"reason_miss":false,"why":""},"lenient":{"caught":true,"false_alarms":0},"points":[{"line":7,"category":"data_model","severity":"problem","text":"Setting user_id as the primary key allows only one notification job per user. Since users receive notifications over time, multiple rows with the same user_id are needed. The primary key should be the notification id, not user_id.","quote":"table db.jobs(user_id pk, id, body, status) index(id)"},{"line":9,"category":"failure_handling","severity":"problem","text":"If a worker crashes after dequeuing a message but before writing to the database, that notification is lost. The queue guarantees at-least-once delivery only up to the worker receiving it, not up to the database record.","quote":"write SEND: client > lb > api ~> queue > worker > db"},{"line":9,"category":"api_contract","severity":"consider","text":"The SEND request returns success when the queue accepts the message, but the notification is only delivered later by a worker. A client cannot confirm whether the notification was actually delivered within the three-minute window.","quote":"write SEND: client > lb > api ~> queue > worker > db"}],"dropped":[],"raw":{"points":[{"category":"data_model","line":7,"severity":"problem","text":"Setting user_id as the primary key allows only one notification job per user. Since users receive notifications over time, multiple rows with the same user_id are needed. The primary key should be the notification id, not user_id."},{"category":"failure_handling","line":9,"severity":"problem","text":"If a worker crashes after dequeuing a message but before writing to the database, that notification is lost. The queue guarantees at-least-once delivery only up to the worker receiving it, not up to the database record."},{"category":"api_co